  "judge_reasoning": "# Evaluation Analysis\n\n## Task Requirement\nCompare the maximum number of private repositories allowed in the **Free** and **Pro** plans in GitHub Pricing.\n\n## Screenshot Evidence\n\n**Screenshots 1, 2, and 5 (GitHub Pricing page):**\n- The \"Compare plans\" table shows columns for **Free ($0 USD)**, **Team ($4 USD)**, and **Enterprise ($21 USD)** — notably, there is **no Pro column** displayed on the current pricing page.\n- The \"Private repositories\" row shows **\"Unlimited\"** for the Free plan (as well as Team and Enterprise).\n\n**Screenshots 3 and 4 (GitHub Docs — \"GitHub's plans\"):**\n- Under \"GitHub Free for personal accounts\": *\"you can work with unlimited collaborators on unlimited public repositories with a full feature set, and on **unlimited private repositories** with a limited feature set.\"*\n- Under \"GitHub Pro\": *\"In addition to the features available with GitHub Free for personal accounts, GitHub Pro includes...\"* — confirming Pro inherits all Free features, including unlimited private repositories.\n\n## Assessment of the Result Response\nThe response states that both Free and Pro plans allow **unlimited private repositories**, making them equal. This conclusion is:\n1. **Consistent with the screenshots** — Free shows \"Unlimited\" private repositories on the pricing page, and the docs confirm unlimited private repositories for Free personal accounts.\n2. **Handles the missing Pro column appropriately** — Since the current GitHub pricing page only displays Free/Team/Enterprise (no Pro column), the agent reasonably supplemented with the official GitHub Docs, which state Pro includes all Free features, thereby verifying Pro also has unlimited private repositories.\n3. **Accurate** — This matches GitHub's actual known plan structure (both Free and Pro offer unlimited private repositories).\n\nThe comparison task was completed: the agent checked the pricing page, identified the Free plan's limit, noticed Pro was absent from the table, verified via official documentation, and delivered a valid comparative conclusion.\n\n## Verdict\n**SUCCESS**",
